{
  "version": "0.7.0",
  "status": "curated",
  "source_ontology": "https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/battery/inferred",
  "notes": [
    "Normative mapping table for descriptor entity-type enrichment.",
    "Keys are normalized to lower-case for deterministic lookup in the JSON-LD renderer.",
    "IRIs verified against EMMO domain-battery 0.21.0, domain-electrochemistry 0.39.0, domain-chemical-substance 0.15.0 and EMMO 1.0.2.",
    "0.3.0: added zn-mno2/alkaline chemistry entries and iec_code section.",
    "0.4.0: added the cell_configuration section. A battery half-cell types as the DEVICE classes BatteryHalfCell (domain-battery, a BatteryCell) and HalfCellDevice (domain-electrochemistry) - never as ElectrochemicalHalfCell, which upstream annotates with an explicit warning against that conflation (it is one electrode plus one electrolyte, not an experimental setup).",
    "0.4.1: cell_configuration gained the three-electrode-cell key, the normalized form of the cell-spec schema's three_electrode_cell enum value; three-electrode stays as the tolerant-import spelling.",
    "0.6.0: chemistry became a two-level vocabulary of electrochemical couples. An entry may name a 'broader' value (li-mno2 is a li-metal system; leclanche is a zinc-carbon system, which is a zn-mno2 system); a record stating the narrower value is also a member of the broader one. Rechargeability stays in the cell-spec 'rechargeable' field and electrode pairing in the electrode basis fields. Entries with 'alias_of' are older spellings that keep resolving but are never recommended. Corrections: zn-mno2 mapped to the alkaline class, which is one of its three variants, and now maps to ZincBattery because domain-battery 0.20.2 has no neutral zinc manganese dioxide class (ZincCarbonBattery is published as the parent of the alkaline class); zinc-carbon mapped to AlkalineCell and now maps to ZincCarbonBattery.",
    "0.7.0: the declared chemistry decides the battery classes. An electrode basis entry names in 'battery_types_chemistry' the chemistry label its battery_types belong to, and adds them only when the cell's chemistry is that label, one broader than it or one narrower than it: graphite, LTO and the lithium-ion positives add their classes to li-ion cells, a lithium negative adds LithiumMetalBattery to li-metal and the couples under it, and MnO2 adds LithiumManganeseDioxideBattery to li-mno2 and li-metal. A chemistry outside the vocabulary declares no class, so the electrode bases add none. Before 0.7.0 every basis added its classes whatever the chemistry, so a zinc cell with an MnO2 positive was typed as a lithium battery. A chemistry entry may name the 'negative_electrode_basis' its couple always has (lithium for li-metal, zinc for the zinc couples); narrower labels inherit it. Validation reports a negative electrode basis that contradicts it, and a lithium or zinc negative electrode under a chemistry that does not name it, unless the cell is a half cell or a three-electrode cell. Name one only for a negative electrode no other couple uses. 'former_node_types' lists electrode classes an entry used to emit, so deposits written with them import to the same basis."
  ],
